**Problem Statement:**
Given a list of integers, determine if it is possible to make all elements in the list a palindrome using only addition and division by 2.

**Java Solution:**

```java
public class Main {
    public static boolean canMakePalindrome(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n / 2; i++) {
            if (nums[i] != nums[n - i - 1]) return false;
            while ((nums[i] & 1) == 0 && nums[i] > 0) nums[i]--;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums1 = {2, 3, 4};
        System.out.println(canMakePalindrome(nums1)); // prints: false

        int[] nums2 = {2, 2, 2};
        System.out.println(canMakePalindrome(nums2)); // prints: true
    }
}
```

**Explanation:**

This solution works by iterating over the input array and checking if each element is equal to its corresponding mirrored element (i.e., `nums[i] == nums[n - i - 1]`). If any pair of elements is not equal, the function immediately returns false.

If all pairs of elements are equal, the function then iterates over the odd-indexed elements in the input array and repeatedly divides them by 2 until they become even. This process effectively "rounds down" these numbers to their nearest lower even number, which should be a palindrome.

The reason why we need to divide by 2 at all is because if an element has an odd number of bits set (i.e., it's not a power of 2), we can't simply remove the last bit and consider the remaining number as the same. Instead, we have to keep dividing until we reach a power of 2.

If the function completes its iteration without finding any unequal pairs or odd numbers that cannot be rounded down to an even palindrome, it returns true, indicating that all elements in the input array can be made into palindromes using only addition and division by 2.
